  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A text message has a $20%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\u223cB(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+\u2026+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809\u2026 \\\\  & \u22480.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
